#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# from_polar_coordinates

#% text
# `from_polar_coordinates([r, theta])` gives the Cartesian point `[r cos(theta), r sin(theta)]`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `from_polar_coordinates([r, t1, ..., tk])` uses hyperspherical coordinates: `x1 = r cos(t1)`, `x2 = r sin(t1) cos(t2)`, ..., the last coordinate `r sin(t1) ... sin(tk)`.

#% item
# A list of points maps over the function.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
from_polar_coordinates([2, pi/3])
#% output
#= [1, 3^(1/2)]

#% cell
from_polar_coordinates([1, a, b])
#% output
#= [cos(a), cos(b)*sin(a), sin(a)*sin(b)]

#% cell
from_polar_coordinates([[1, 0], [2, pi/2]])
#% output
#= [[1, 0], [0, 2]]

